WEBVTT

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.579
In 1908, the sky over Siberia exploded.

00:00:03.589 --> 00:00:05.887
On June 30, reindeer herders saw a

00:00:05.897 --> 00:00:08.105
fireball and a brilliant flash.

00:00:08.115 --> 00:00:09.605
Then came a blast, and a hot

00:00:09.615 --> 00:00:10.415
wind.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:12.521
One man was thrown through the air.

00:00:12.531 --> 00:00:14.809
Trees were flattened across more than two

00:00:14.819 --> 00:00:17.215
thousand square kilometres of forest.

00:00:17.225 --> 00:00:19.409
When a scientist finally reached the spot

00:00:19.419 --> 00:00:22.982
in 1927, he expected a huge crater.

00:00:22.992 --> 00:00:24.482
There was no crater at the centre

00:00:24.492 --> 00:00:25.607
of the blast.

00:00:25.617 --> 00:00:27.393
Just a shattered forest.

00:00:27.403 --> 00:00:29.628
The object, most likely a space rock,

00:00:29.638 --> 00:00:32.353
had exploded in the air,
several kilometres

00:00:32.363 --> 00:00:32.967
up.

00:00:32.977 --> 00:00:35.702
Its energy is often estimated at ten

00:00:35.712 --> 00:00:38.211
to fifteen megatons of TNT.

00:00:38.221 --> 00:00:40.510
Because the area was so remote, only

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:41.797
a few people are known to have

00:00:41.807 --> 00:00:42.572
died.

00:00:42.582 --> 00:00:45.907
Now, June 30 is
International Asteroid Day.
